Subject: [PATCH 039/154] feat: sweep the leaf files clear of the dead bridge
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Four files whose bridge calls had no cross-file cascade, plus the settings
menu hook, which had one and is better gone.

Extensions and autocomplete: the dylib push goes, the HTML feed stays.
PushActionsToHtmlArm, PushMatchesToPage and both icon caches are what the
pill and the palette actually read since 444.2 and 444.3, and each one sits
one line from a call that is now dead. ExtIconCache::Get().Store is the same
shape as the FaviconCache trap and was one line above the deleted push.

The autocomplete bridge loses its whole Swift payload builder - MatchToJson
and its two helpers, 164 lines. atrium_command_bar_matches.cc has built the
HTML rows since 444.2, so this was a second encoder for a reader that no
longer exists. query_generation_ went with the streaming envelope that was
its only reader.

The sidebar producer loses its first source. CopySidebarModelJSON returns
empty in every build, so the Rust core already answered every call; the
comment claiming Swift is the real producer outlived the fact by a day.

The settings menu hook is deleted rather than reduced. Its whole purpose was
to re-point the Settings item at a Swift window, and forwarding instead would
leave the item always-enabled where stock gates it on canOpenNewBrowser - a
difference with nothing behind it. Removing the hook restores stock exactly.
A follow-up rebuilds the surface and carries the reason the item needs its own
hook at all: main_menu_builder gives it an explicit target and action, so the
IDC_OPTIONS intercept never sees the key.

ExtensionActionBridge loses its AtriumHost parameter, which nothing read
after the push went.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
